#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# bd-divergence — RETIRED 2026-08-05 (CX-hrbn / CX-dzfv).
#
# This measured how far the `bd` CLI store had drifted from the
# substrate `/bd/` ticket-DAG, back when tickets could be filed through
# either surface and nothing reconciled them (CX-jhvn).
#
# The tix-authority migration ended that: all 798 bd tickets now live in
# the substrate tracker, and bd is archive. Nothing writes bd any more,
# so this script's central number stopped meaning what it says. Every
# ticket filed on the substrate from here on shows up as "substrate-only
# divergence" — the report would grow without bound while the underlying
# condition it was built to detect no longer exists. A frozen store
# cannot diverge; it can only be left behind.
#
# Use bin/tix-drift instead. It compares the surfaces that are still
# live.
#
# The original body is in git history — `git log --follow -- bin/bd-divergence`.
# The file is kept as a signpost so muscle-memory invocations land here
# rather than on "command not found".

cat >&2 <<'MSG'
bd-divergence — RETIRED (2026-08-05).

bd is archive: the tix-authority migration moved all 798 tickets to the
substrate tracker, and nothing writes bd any more. A divergence scan
against a frozen store reports noise — substrate-side growth reads as
drift, forever, while the two-writer condition this was built to catch
is gone.

Use instead:

  bin/tix-drift

Design: /home/jes/commonplace-plan/docs/plans/2026-08-05-tix-authority-migration-design.md §5-§8
History: git log --follow -- bin/bd-divergence
MSG

exit 2
